#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065119} :activity/id #uuid "56a150ec-8529-5db2-95d7-052f09baf4bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375 :topic/id #uuid "9a4e8d0a-b62a-512a-8fb3-08502110771a" :topic/math-academy-id 372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065449 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}.$\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that \n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6),$ and\n\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:**  We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$\n\nThe factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6), \\qquad (2,3).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:**  Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+6 & =7 & ✓ \\\\ 2+3 & =5 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:**  Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:**  Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3 & = \\\\ x(2x+1)+3(2x+1) & = \\\\ (2x+1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! \n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+1)(x+3) & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot (x+3)+1\\cdot (x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6x+x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7e6d3a2-936b-5775-9cf9-10135f0e6486" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9455 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c655bc80-5885-54d2-8a94-0ad9c1a41019" :step/math-academy-id 177992 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064247 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064002 :question/id #uuid "44cf8c85-f228-5eaa-922d-613355cb7d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483430}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "462ef775-e55e-588f-acaf-4bf8f7bdc2df" :step/math-academy-id 177993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :question/id #uuid "35bedd9f-bcb7-5024-bfe7-5ef6d34518bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-15073"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cfe2121-66f0-53fa-b744-041fe830bf13" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483415}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants"} :step/id #uuid "1a35042e-19b9-53df-b251-f234f0733094" :step/math-academy-id 177997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065450 :tutorial/content "To factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant that adds to the linear coefficient.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}y^2 {\\color{purple}{\\,-\\,7}}y + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant, ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6$, is *positive*, while\n\n- the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}})$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-6), \\quad (-2,-3).\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their sum is *negative*, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we determine which of these factor pairs sums to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-6) & =-7\\,✓ \\\\ (-2)+(-3) & =-5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-y - 6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-7y+3 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-y-6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-y)-(6y-3) & = \\\\ y(2y-1)-3(2y-1) & = \\\\ (y-3)(2y-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ff88dae-40df-5a63-a7bd-0d73317e94a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9506 :tutorial/title "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "412ba190-4d83-5be1-abb2-91fae26ca40e" :step/math-academy-id 178001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :question/id #uuid "ad032833-ecf8-5295-9a9f-f69f759c19b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483361}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants"} :step/id #uuid "e94449ef-31f1-5a5f-b3b9-9cc8fc6af17b" :step/math-academy-id 178002}] :activity/title "Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064458}]}
